item.description:
In reply to &lt;a href=&quot;/2017/01/04/the-tradeoff/comment-page-1/#comment-1704189&quot;&gt;Grant Shalks&lt;/a&gt;.

&lt;p&gt;Hi Grant, Good question...&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
I&#8217;m confused by &#8220;Nature is not reducible to polar opposites&#8221;.&lt;br /&gt; Surely if anything characterizes nature, polar opposites do: dark / light, hot cold, fertile/infertile etc.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;First, let’s review: In &lt;u&gt;The Principles of Quantum Mechanics &lt;/u&gt; (1930), Paul Dirac contrasted the traditional common sense Newtonian world and the quantum one: “&lt;em&gt;It has become increasingly evident… that nature works on a different plan. Her fundamental laws do not govern the world as it appears in our mental picture in any direct way, but instead they control a substratum of which &lt;u&gt;we cannot form a mental picture without introducing irrelevancies&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our words and language form much of this &lt;u&gt;&lt;em&gt;mental picture&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/u&gt; and thereby &lt;u&gt;&lt;em&gt;introduce irrelevancies&lt;/em&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/u&gt;. More precisely, the words and language we use to think about the world arise from the electro-chemical ON / OFF nature of the nervous system (neurons). This biology forces us to perceive reality in a dipolar way. While this ON/OFF process is just a speck of nature&#039;s cosmic reality, this &lt;u&gt;speck is the primary process &lt;/u&gt; life relies on to perceive and maneuver through the environment (i.e. survive). &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;  In other words, survival rather than perceiving nature&#039;s cosmic reality is the driving force of evolution. Obviously, our large brain fosters vast and subtle features of this ON/OFF process compared with protozoa or plants, for example. Alas, that advantage allows human language to exacerbate this dipolar perceptual bias.This link should retrieve some information on this dipolar situation. &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/?s=dipolar&quot;&gt;https://centertao.local/?s=dipolar&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;

Chapter 1 addresses this language problem right off the bat... &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-1/&quot;&gt;The way possible to think, runs counter to the constant way.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-1/&quot;&gt;The name possible to express runs counter to the constant name.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; Chapter 70 offers a hint too...&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-70/&quot;&gt;Our words are very easy to know, very easy to do.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-70/&quot;&gt;Under heaven none can know, none can do.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-70/&quot;&gt;Speech has its faction, involvement has its sovereign.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; Of course, our brains are wired for language, words, which depict nature in the only way words can, dipolar. When you loose your faith in the words you use to define the world you see, you become freer to see the world, nature, as it actually may be. Chapter 71 bluntly reveals this perceptual problem, and a way to manage it.&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-71/&quot;&gt;Realizing I don&#8217;t&#8217; know is better; not knowing this knowing is disease.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; I hope this helps a little. I&#039;d just say, this Taoist thought journey takes a lifetime to travel and realizations trickle out over that time span.  That&#039;s a good thing. I mean that is one fine benefit of aging. The only one, I suppose. :)&lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt; carl
&lt;/p&gt;
